# ==== INLINE-HEADER COMMENT · the deferred injection's two control cells
#
# An inline suite's header comment is CLAIMED before the body renders and
# EMITTED after, at the output line the claim recorded. That back-injection has
# two consequences the aligner can see, and each gets a cell.
#
# CELL 1 — MUST-NOT-MOVE control: a SINGLE-LINE inline body. The header comment
#   is already at the buffer's tail when it is emitted, so resolving the
#   recorded anchor must land it in exactly the same place it always did. This
#   is the regression guard for the multi-line cell's fix.
#
# CELL 2 — SILENT MISALIGNMENT: a multi-line inline body whose statements carry
#   a trailing-comment GROUP. Those entries are recorded BEFORE the header's
#   (the body renders first), at absolute buffer offsets, and the header's
#   comment is then spliced in AHEAD of them. Two things must happen or the
#   aligner writes spaces into the middle of a line: every later entry's offset
#   shifts by the spliced length, and the header's own entry is inserted at its
#   SORTED position rather than pushed onto the end (the planner walks its scan
#   cursor forward once and would otherwise mis-attribute every entry after
#   it). Either half alone leaves one failure mode.
#
# Both are FIXPOINT cells. The alignment of the body group is the assertion:
# the two `case` comments share a column, and neither line is corrupted.

void single_line_body(int x):
    if x == 1: print(1)    # control: single-line body, comment does not move

int multi_line_body(int y):
    if true: int x = match y:    # note: the header's own comment
        case 2:
            int a = 1       # group member one
            int bb = 22     # group member two
            20
        else:
            30
    return 0

void main():
    single_line_body(1)
    print(multi_line_body(2))
